Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_1.md. I judged the diff and did not re-run the build or test suite, since the harness had already run both and they passed.

What the fix does: TruncatingTool now keeps the read_file result from before truncation. If the lines the model actually gets differ from the original, it calls partial_read_guard::retract_cut, which removes the page the guard had just recorded. So a whole-file write_file after a cut page is refused.

Why I'm satisfied it works:
• No wrapper re-ordering: the wrapper order in src/tools.rs is unchanged, as the task preferred.
• Coverage still adds up: the guard now stores read ranges unmerged so it can remove exactly one page. That's safe because the coverage check (covers_all) and the refusal text still merge the ranges before using them.
• Tests run through the real stack: they use with_truncation(maybe_guard(ReadFileTool)) at the real 30,000-character limit and cover all four required cases:
  • a cut whole-file page leads to a refused write;
  • a page under the limit is admitted as before;
  • a cut page followed by four 50-line pages is admitted;
  • the refusal names the path, offset/limit and a smaller limit.
• One extra test shows a cut withdraws only its own page and leaves earlier reads alone.
• Refusal is actionable: the message now says a truncated page doesn't count and to use a smaller limit, so the model has a way out instead of looping.
• Nothing left unused: first_text, retract_cut_with and retract_cut are all called within this diff. The size cap for src/tool_wrappers.rs was raised (5713 → 5724) with a stated reason.
• Docs match: docs/src/troubleshooting/safety.md, the ARCHITECTURE.md entry (item 2 fixed; items 1, 3 and 4 open) and the module comment were all updated.

